\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Precision and recall values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Precision and recall are evaluation metrics used to assess the performance of a machine learning model.
 Precision expresses the proportion of predicted positive cases that are actually correct,
 while recall indicates the proportion of true positive cases that the model successfully identifies.
 Together,
 these metrics provide essential insight into the effectiveness of a model in classification tasks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Precision is calculated as:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Precision}=\frac{TruePositives}{TruePositives+FalsePositives}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Precision reflects the model’s ability to avoid false positives,
 making it a key indicator of prediction reliability.
 In other words,
 the higher the value,
 the fewer the errors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Recall value confirms the model’s ability to correctly identify the positive class,
 reflecting how many of the actual positive instances were successfully detected.
 As with precision and recall,
 the higher the value,
 the more successful the classification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall is calculated as:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\text{Recall}=\frac{TruePositives}{TruePositives+FaulseNegatives}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Attica precision and recall.
