\begin_layout Section
Conclusions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This research developed new wildfire danger rules following an in depth examination of the environmental characteristics associated with each recorded fire incident and the corresponding analytical outcomes.
 For the purposes of the study,
 we employed several machine learning approaches,
 including Random Forest (RF),
 a set of feature construction techniques such as FC2 (RF) and FC2 (MLP),
 along with artificial Neural Network like RBF and MLP (BFGS) and finally GENCLASS a portable tool for data classification based on Grammatical Evolution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the completion of the project,
 we used climatological and operational firefighting data from 2000 to 2024 for three different regions of Greece,
 in order to highlight the distinct climatological conditions characterizing each of them.
 This perspective underscores that every region is unique and must be investigated according to its own climatological particularities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our results indicated stable performance across all datasets.
 Specifically,
 for the Attica dataset,
 GENCLASS achieved the best performance with the lowest error rate of 12.43%.
 It was followed by the feature construction techniques FC2 (RF) and FC2 (MLP),
 the artificial neural network MLP (BFGS),
 the Random Forest (RF),
 while RBF ranked last in terms of accuracy.
 In the precision and recall values,
 GENCLASS achieved the highest performance,
 ranging between 88.50% and 88.10%,
 respectively,
 while RBF ranked last with corresponding values of 85.43% and 85.32%.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dataset for Eastern Macedonia & Thrace showed that the feature construction technique FC2 (MLP) achieved the best performance,
 with an error rate of 11.87%,
 outperforming the other methods by a small margin of 0.06%.
 In second place,
 GENCLASS and the feature construction technique FC2 (RF) tied,
 both recording an error rate of 11.93%.
 The artificial neural network RBF ranked last in this dataset,
 yielding an error rate of 15.34%.
 In the precision values,
 Random Forest (RF) achieved the best performance with 89.45%,
 followed by the artificial neural network MLP (BFGS) with 89.04%,
 and GENCLASS with 89%.
 RBF ranked last,
 yielding a precision of 83.51%.
 Regarding recall,
 GENCLASS achieved the highest value at 88.90%.
 In second place,
 FC2 (RF) and FC2 (MLP) tied,
 both reaching 89.08%.
 Once again,
 RBF exhibited the lowest performance,
 with a recall value of 83.82%.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the Crete dataset,
 GENCLASS achieved the best error accuracy with 15.11%,
 followed by FC2 (MLP) with 15.56%,
 while RBF ranked last with an error rate of 17.82%.
 In the precision and recall values,
 GENCLASS obtained the highest performance,
 ranging between 85.07% and 85.50%.
 In terms of precision,
 GENCLASS tied with Random Forest (RF) at 85.07%,
 whereas in recall RF ranked second with 85.03%.
 Finally,
 the RBF technique recorded the lowest performance in both precision and recall,
 with values of 82.09%.
\end_layout

